--TEST--
An anonymous object `{name: value}` builds a methodless object whose fields read back by name (`rule:types/anonymous-object` and `rule:types/erased-member-access`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/anonymous-object`: each anonymous object's precise type is a compiler-synthesized class
// with exactly the fields written, each field's type inferred from its own
// initializer. § 4: a field the shape names is proven present, so reading it
// is a plain slot load that never throws.
var $p = {x: 1, y: 2};
echo $p->x, " ", $p->y, "\n";

// A field's slot comes from the shape's *sorted* field list, not from the
// order the object wrote them in — the two sides of the read agree because
// both count through the same sorted names.
var $t = {zed: "z", alpha: "a"};
echo $t->alpha, $t->zed, "\n";

// § 2: an ordinary object in every other respect, so a refcounted field owns
// what it holds and the value read out of it outlives the read.
var $who = "grace";
var $s = {greeting: Core\Str::upper("hi"), who: $who};
echo $s->greeting, " ", $s->who, " ", $who, "\n";

// Two anonymous objects with the same fields are two instances of one shape, with
// reference semantics rather than a copy: `$b` is a second object.
var $a = {n: 1};
var $b = {n: 2};
echo $a->n, $b->n, "\n";

// A field's value is an expression, so an anonymous object nests.
var $n = {inner: {v: 7}, tag: "deep"};
echo $n->tag, " ", $n->inner->v, "\n";
--EXPECT--
1 2
az
HI grace grace
12
deep 7
